"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[9943],{5361(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Mergesort","permalink":"/concepts/tags/mergesort","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":2,"items":[{"id":"fundamental-problem-domains/efficient-sorting","title":"Beyond Quadratic Sorting","description":"Quicksort is not the only way to sort faster than $O(n^2)$. Each of the three basic ideas (exchange, insertion and selection) has an efficient counterpart, and there are also algorithms based on completely different principles. This lesson presents the most important ones, explains the theoretical limit that all comparison-based algorithms share, and shows how some algorithms manage to go below that limit.","permalink":"/concepts/fundamental-problem-domains/efficient-sorting"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-2","title":"Divide and Conquer: Classic Problems","description":"This lesson presents some of the most famous divide and conquer algorithms. Two of them, Mergesort and Quicksort, we already know from the sorting chapter; now we can analyze them with the formulas of this chapter. The others (the Towers of Hanoi, the multiplication of large integers, the tennis tournament and the Tromino puzzle) show how the paradigm solves problems that would be hard to approach in any other way.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-2"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);